432:**What the repo already does** (re-read at 43f3e57; the lines revision 1 cited are where it said). It imports a five-column CSV or a Monzo export with strict validation (src/cma/ingest.py, src/cma/importers/monzo.py). An ordered regex categoriser never guesses (src/cma/categorise.py). No rule mentions cash, so a cash deposit today falls to OTHE
434:On the T03 branch (cited at ecec4ca for revision 4; not merged) the v2 table is data with a stage and a protected flag on every rule, cash paid in is no longer protected, and the payment rail is a cue with no category (src/cma/rules/v2.yaml:117-122 and 214-236 on that branch). A credit's verdict is computed in two stages, and an ambiguous credi
447:| Genuine trading income separated from transfers, director money and cash | Revenue is claimed narrowly; every other credit, cash included, is one OTHER pile in M16 | No breakdown of money in; cash takings, director loans, own-account transfers and refunds look alike |
448:| Real acquirers and merchant cash advances recognised | Only synthetic acquirer names (README:125-126) | Real card revenue lands in M16 and refers the case |
470:**Sub-decision: how to show what is unexplained.** (i) Redefine M16 to mean unexplained only. (ii) Keep M16's value (money in that is not trading receipts), rename it to say so, and add a figure for each bucket plus the unexplained share (M26). *Chose (ii)*: a memo already signed that cites M16 must keep its meaning, and ids are stable.
514:**Sub-decision: which category a credit gets inside one bucket (revision 4).** Two rules can agree on the bucket but carry different categories: a gateway (card settlement) and an invoice reference (customer transfer) are both trading receipts; a returned-payment cue and a refund cue are both refunds and returned payments. The category matters 
701:- A judgement queue in `cma run`'s terminal review (revision 4). `cma run` is not a case: under v2 it records each ambiguous credit's conflict and candidates in its evidence lines and counts the credit in M16, and the queue belongs to the workspace (R15). `cma run` has no profile, so its candidates never include a profile name, and no credit is
774:  - M16 is renamed so it cannot be confused with M26 (unexplained); M18 is the cash inside M2, not a bucket beside it; [R1] to [R9] in a pack are policy rules, not requirement ids.
820:**New figures.** No id is ever reused. M1-M16 keep their ids and units. M16 is renamed 'Non-trading money in (share)'; M2 keeps the name Total revenue, and its method sentence now names cash paid in. Values move only on lines a v2 rule now recognises or finds ambiguous (R10).
837:Reconciliation: M17 = M2 + M19 + M20 + M21 + M22 + M23 + M24 + M25, in pence. M18 is part of M2, is not added again, and its evidence is a subset of M2's. M16 and M26 share one denominator: money in less lending drawdowns, returned direct debits and own-account transfers. Cash deposits are trading receipts, so they are in neither numerator; an 
845:**Policy check.** PolicyResult and policy.evaluate do not change. The workspace and the pack evaluate only the packaged policy. The label is one constant with no digits in it, so it never trips the guard: 'Illustrative thresholds shipped with this tool. They are not your firm's lending policy, and this is not a lending decision: the underwriter
932:**Graph and prompt.** Unchanged, except that the metrics table has more rows and M16 has its new name. FakeLLM parses rows by id and is unaffected; its sentence about M16 is reworded. Under v2 the evidence lines `cma run` writes carry each line's rule, the conflict form included; the terminal review shows no queue.
1009:| `cma run` meets an ambiguous credit under v2 | Its evidence line records the conflict and every table candidate, and the credit counts in M16; `cma run` has no profile, so no profile candidate and no profile-made ambiguity; the terminal review shows no queue (out of scope; the queue is the workspace's) | R4, R53 |
1094:- Evidence JSON v1 is a superset of today's record. Records without schema_version are read as v0; verify recomputes M1-M16 for them and says that is all it can check.
1095:- The committed examples (examples/cafe_stretched.memo.md, .memo.evidence.json and .truth.json) are regenerated in the same change. The CSV is byte-identical; the appendix gains M17-M28, M16's new name and M2's reworded method; in the truth file, the director capital line becomes director_funding. test_categoriser_matches_truth runs under v2; 
1123:- Policy YAML: R9's description (uk_sme_default.yaml:86, 'left unexplained') is reworded to match M16's new name, and the policy hash changes, recorded as before. R9 keeps M16. Whether it should move to M26 is a decision for whoever owns the firm's policy. The workspace and pack read the same packaged file, so there is no second policy to migr
